Kubernetes Concepts
________________________________________
1. Node Selector, Node Affinity, Pod Affinity, Pod Anti-Affinity, Taints, and Tolerations
1.1 Node Selector
•	NodeSelector is the simplest way to assign a pod to a specific node.
•	Pods will run only on nodes that have matching labels.
•	It’s a hard requirement: if no node matches, the pod remains pending.
•	Example:
nodeSelector:
  disktype: ssd
•	Meaning: Pod will only run on nodes labeled disktype=ssd.
________________________________________
1.2 Node Affinity
•	Node Affinity is a more flexible version of NodeSelector.
•	Types:
1.	Required (hard rule): Pod must run on matching node.
2.	Preferred (soft rule): Scheduler will try to place pod on preferred nodes, but can place it elsewhere if needed.
•	Example:
requiredDuringSchedulingIgnoredDuringExecution:
  nodeSelectorTerms:
    - matchExpressions:
        - key: disktype
          operator: In
          values:
            - ssd
________________________________________
1.3 Pod Affinity
•	Pod Affinity allows pods to run near other pods.
•	Useful for reducing latency between related components.
•	Example use case: Backend pod runs on the same node as frontend pod.
________________________________________
1.4	Pod Anti-Affinity
•	Pod Anti-Affinity ensures pods do not run near certain pods.
•	Useful for high availability or spreading replicas.
•	Example use case: Avoid placing two database replicas on the same node.
________________________________________
1.5 Taints and Tolerations
•	Taints are applied to nodes to repel pods.
•	Tolerations are applied to pods to allow them to run on tainted nodes.
•	Example:
o	Taint: db=true:NoSchedule → only pods that tolerate this taint can run.
o	Toleration:
tolerations:
- key: "db"
   operator: "Equal"
   value: "true"
   effect: "NoSchedule"
•	Use case: Dedicated node for MySQL pods; no other pods can run there.
