3. Deployment and Pod Lifecycle
Scenario: Deployment runs multiple pods; one pod fails.
Step-by-step explanation:
1.	Pod crashes
o	Detected by kubelet on the node.
2.	kubelet → API Server
o	Reports pod status to the API server.
3.	API Server → etcd
o	Stores the current state of the pod in etcd.
4.	Controller Manager
o	Watches API server.
o	Sees: desired replicas ≠ current replicas.
o	Creates a new Pod object (spec only, no node assigned yet).
5.	Scheduler
o	Assigns the new pod to a suitable node based on resource availability.
o	Updates API server with node assignment.
6.	kubelet on assigned node
o	Pulls container image and starts the pod.
o	Reports status to API server.
7.	kube-proxy
o	Not involved in pod creation; only handles networking.
Interview-ready one-liner:
When a pod fails, kubelet reports it to the API server, the controller manager creates a replacement pod, the scheduler assigns it to a node, and kubelet starts the pod.
