4. Horizontal Pod Autoscaler (HPA) and Node Autoscaling
4.1 Pod Autoscaling (HPA)
•	HPA automatically scales pods based on metrics (CPU, memory, or custom metrics).
•	Requires Metrics Server to collect pod resource usage.
•	Without Metrics Server, HPA cannot scale.
•	Example:
apiVersion: autoscaling/v2
kind: HorizontalPodAutoscaler
spec:
  scaleTargetRef:
    apiVersion: apps/v1
    kind: Deployment
    name: my-app
  minReplicas: 2
  maxReplicas: 10
  metrics:
  - type: Resource
    resource:
      name: cpu
      target:
        type: Utilization
        averageUtilization: 70
4.2 Node Autoscaling
•	Node autoscaling is different from HPA.
•	Setting min/max nodes in a node group is required, but not sufficient.
•	Requires Cluster Autoscaler.
•	Flow:
1.	HPA increases pods.
2.	Pending pods cannot be scheduled due to insufficient resources.
3.	Cluster Autoscaler adds new nodes automatically.
4.	Scheduler places pods on new nodes.
Important: Labels on nodes can help identify node groups for autoscaling.
________________________________________
4.3 HPA and Memory Scaling Limitations
•	HPA works best with CPU metrics, not memory.
•	Reason:
o	Memory usage is sticky: containers rarely release memory.
o	Metrics may be delayed, causing scaling to react too late.
o	Late scaling can lead to OOMKilled pods.
•	Solution:
o	Prefer CPU-based HPA.
o	Combine HPA + Cluster Autoscaler.
o	Use Vertical Pod Autoscaler (VPA) to scale pod memory.
Interview one-liner:
HPA doesn’t scale memory well because memory usage is sticky and not easily released, making scaling reactive and late. CPU-based scaling is much more responsive.
